{
  "en": {
    "title": "Why I built tt",
    "summary": "I've written TypeScript for years, and I still like it. So this isn't a complaint about TypeScript. It's a note about one place where I kept getting stuck, and what I ended up doing about it.",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "The escape hatches are load-bearing"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "TypeScript's first job is to accept JavaScript that already exists. That isn't a footnote in its design; it's the foundation. "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "any"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " and "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "as"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " are there because a type system layered on top of a decade of running code needs somewhere to put the parts it can't prove."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Most of us learn to avoid them. And the moment you decide to avoid them seriously, you notice where the cost moves: you start writing types whose only job is to make other types work. Conditional types, recursive tuple types, overload tables. The type-level code grows until it feels like a second language living inside the annotations."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "The pipe operator is the example I keep coming back to. TypeScript has no "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "|>"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", so libraries approximate it with a variadic "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "pipe(...)"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ". Open any of them and you'll find the same thing — a stack of hand-written overloads, one per arity, usually stopping somewhere around ten or twenty:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "label": "typescript",
        "html": "<span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">export</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> function</span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\"> pipe</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">&#x3C;</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">A</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">B</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">>(</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">a</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> A</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, </span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\">ab</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> (</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">a</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> A</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">) </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">=></span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">)</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">;</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">export</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> function</span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\"> pipe</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">&#x3C;</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">A</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">B</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">C</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">>(</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">a</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> A</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, </span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\">ab</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> (</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">a</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> A</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">) </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">=></span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, </span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\">bc</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> (</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">b</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">) </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">=></span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> C</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">)</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> C</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">;</span><br><span style=\"color:#8B949E\">// ...and on, and on</span>"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "It works right up until it doesn't. Go one step past the last overload and the error you get is about the library's overload list, not about your program. That's nobody's mistake. The authors did the best available thing. The limit is structural: you're asking a type system to describe a syntax the language doesn't have, and a description is only ever an approximation of the real thing."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "The compiler knows, but doesn't say"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "The other thing that nagged at me was the silence."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "label": "typescript",
        "html": "<span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">type</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> Shape</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> =</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">  |</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> { </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">kind</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#A5D6FF\"> \"circle\"</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">; </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">radius</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> number</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> }</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">  |</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> { </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">kind</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#A5D6FF\"> \"rect\"</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">; </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">width</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> number</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">; </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">height</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> number</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> };</span><br><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">function</span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\"> area</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">(</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">shape</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> Shape</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">) {</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">  switch</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> (shape.kind) {</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">    case</span><span style=\"color:#A5D6FF\"> \"circle\"</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">:</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">      return</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> Math.</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\">PI</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> *</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> shape.radius </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">**</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> 2</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">;</span><br><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">  }</span><br><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">}</span>"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "TypeScript knows "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "shape.kind"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " is exactly "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "\"circle\" | \"rect\""
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", and it knows "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "\"rect\""
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " isn't handled here. The information is sitting right there in the narrowing. But you won't hear about it unless you ask, and asking means writing the ritual — the "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "default"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " branch that assigns the value to a "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "never"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", or an explicit return type plus the right compiler flags. Write the same "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "case"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " twice and the compiler says nothing at all; catching that is left to a lint rule."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Given everything TypeScript has to be, none of this is unreasonable. It's just that in most languages with tagged unions, this check is a feature of the language, and here it's a pattern I have to remember to write — one more piece of type machinery whose only purpose is to make the type system speak up."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "What I actually wanted"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Products keep getting more demanding, and the code follows. When a function grows past what fits in your head, the things that save you are pretty simple: knowing exactly what goes in and what comes out, having as many mistakes as possible caught before the code runs, and being able to say all that without three screens of ceremony."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "That's the whole motivation. I wanted those three properties inside TypeScript — not by getting cleverer with types, but by moving the work one step earlier, into a compile step."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "Why not one of the existing languages"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Languages like ReScript already solved this, and solved it well. But they solve it by compiling their own syntax down to JavaScript, and that's the hurdle. You aren't adopting a feature; you're stepping outside the ecosystem, at least at the boundary. Types for the packages you already use, editor tooling, the code your team wrote last year, the next person you hire. For a codebase with a lot of TypeScript in it, that trade is hard to justify no matter how good the language is."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Meanwhile TypeScript stopped being an optional layer. Bun runs it natively; Node has type stripping; type-first APIs are the norm. TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript, and by now it's the part of the ecosystem you can't really opt out of."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "So I went the other way. tt is a superset of TypeScript that compiles "
          },
          {
            "kind": "em",
            "text": "to"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " TypeScript — not to JavaScript. TypeScript stays the target, the type checker, and the thing everything else in your project already understands."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "The one rule everything else hangs off"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "strong",
            "text": "Every valid TypeScript file is a valid tt file."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "The compiler transforms only the syntax it owns and passes everything else through byte for byte. What it emits is plain TypeScript — "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "kind"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "-tagged unions, "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "switch"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " statements, ordinary functions. No runtime library, no type tricks. If tt has something to say, it says it itself, with a file, line, and column."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "That rule buys two things. Learning tt is learning a handful of keywords, not a language. And migration doesn't have to be a project: rename a file and it compiles. Because "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": ".tt"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " and "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": ".ts"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " import each other in both directions, you can also just... not migrate. Write the part that matters — a state machine, a parser, a payment flow — in tt, and use it from the TypeScript you already have."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Here's the "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "switch"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " from earlier:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "label": "example.tt",
        "html": "<span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">enum</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> Shape</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> {</span><br><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\">  Circle</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">(radius</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> number</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">),</span><br><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\">  Rect</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">(width</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> number</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, height</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> number</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">),</span><br><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">}</span><br><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">const</span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\"> area</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> =</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> (</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">shape</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> Shape</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">)</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> number</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> =></span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">  match</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> (shape) {</span><br><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\">    Circle</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">(radius) </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">=></span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> Math.</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\">PI</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> *</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> radius </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">**</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> 2</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">,</span><br><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">  };</span>"
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "label": "output",
        "html": "<span>error[match-not-exhaustive]: match on enum Shape is not exhaustive: missing \"Rect\"<br> --> shape.tt:7:3<br>  |<br>7 |   match (shape) {<br>  |   ^^^^^^^^^^^^^<br>  |<br>  = help: add the missing arms or a final `_` arm</span>"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Write "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "Circle"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " twice and you get "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "match: duplicate arm \"Circle\""
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ". Same information the type system had all along — it just gets said out loud now."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "Errors you can read off a signature"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "The other half is failure. tt leans on returned errors rather than thrown ones, and gives the ergonomics that usually make people give up on that style:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "label": "example.tt",
        "html": "<span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">const</span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\"> loadProfile</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> =</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> (</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">id</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#79C0FF\"> string</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">)</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">:</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> TResult</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">&#x3C;</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">Profile</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">, </span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\">DbError</span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\"> |</span><span style=\"color:#FFA657\"> HttpError</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">> </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">=></span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">  result</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> {</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">    const</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> user </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">&#x3C;-</span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\"> findUser</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">(id);</span><br><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">    const</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\"> company </span><span style=\"color:#FF7B72\">&#x3C;-</span><span style=\"color:#D2A8FF\"> fetchCompany</span><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">(user.companyId);</span><br><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">    { user, company }</span><br><span style=\"color:#E6EDF3\">  };</span>"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "Each "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "<-"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " unwraps a success and short-circuits a failure, and the error types union themselves as you go. It reads like the happy path because it is the happy path, but the failures are in the signature rather than in a comment or a "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "catch"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " three frames up."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "tt doesn't forbid throwing — it's still TypeScript, and "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "throw"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " is still there when you want it. It just makes the other style comfortable enough to actually use."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "The part I didn't expect to care about"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "A lot of code gets written with AI now, and what decides whether that goes well is context. Not \"enough\" context — the "
          },
          {
            "kind": "em",
            "text": "right"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " context, in the window the model can actually see."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "That turned out to be the same property I wanted for myself. A "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "match"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " is the complete list of cases, right there. A signature returning "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "TResult<Profile, DbError | HttpError>"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " is the complete list of failures, right there. Neither answer lives in another file, or in a "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "throw"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " several frames down, or in a convention someone explained in a meeting. A model reading forty lines of tt can tell you what those forty lines do, and so can a reviewer at 11pm."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "Where this is"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "tt is early. The compiler is written in Rust, the language is seven constructs and one binding modifier — "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "enum"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "match"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "try"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "let-else"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "if let"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": ", "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "|>"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " (with "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "flow"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "), "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "result"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " blocks, and "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "val"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": " — and I wouldn't put it in production yet."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "But the shape of it is what I wanted: nothing to give up, a few things to learn, and a compiler that tells you what your types already knew."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "ko": {
    "title": "tt를 만든 이유",
    "summary": "저는 여러 해 동안 TypeScript를 사용해 왔고, 지금도 TypeScript를 좋아합니다. 그러니 이 글은 TypeScript에 대한 불평이 아닙니다. 제가 계속 막히곤 했던 한 지점과, 결국 그 문제를 어떻게 풀게 되었는지에 관한 이야기입니다.",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "타입 탈출구는 설계를 떠받치고 있습니다"
      },
      {
        "kind": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "TypeScript의 첫 번째 임무는 이미 존재하는 JavaScript를 받아들이는 것입니다. 이는 설계의 사소한 각주가 아니라 토대입니다. "
          },
          {
            "kind": "code",
            "text": "any"
